<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n113154" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χαλκό-πους</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ποδος</span>,
			ὁ, ἡ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>with feet of bronze,</i>
			
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τρίπους</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					E.
					<u>Supp.</u>
					1197
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀδός,</span>
			<i>founded on bronze,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>OC</u>
				57
			</span></i> (expld. by Sch. with ref. to <i>copper</i>-mines): in <i><span class="en">
				Hom.
			</span></i> of horses, to express the solid strength of their hoofs, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χαλκόποδ᾽ ἵππω</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Il.
					8.41
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ταῦροι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Pherecyd.
					112J.
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χ. Ἐρινύς,</span> to express her
			untiring pursuit, <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>El.</u>
				491
			</span></i> (lyr.); of Empedocles, <i>with bronze slippers,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Luc.
				<u>DMort.</u>
				20.4
			</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
